ref="/component-react">Component React</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/constructor-in-functional">Constructor in Functional</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/convert-string-to-component-react">Convert String to Component React</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/react-in-css">React in Css</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/react-devtools-extension">React Devtools Extension</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/react-developer-salary">React Developer Salary</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/react-google-login">React Google Login</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/react-developer-resume">React Developer Resume</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/conditional-css-in-react">Conditional CSS in React</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/vue-vs-react">Vue vs React</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/usestate-hook-in-react-js">UseState Hook in React.js</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/class-components-in-react-js">Class Components in React JS</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/react-basics">React Basics</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/reactjs-compiler">ReactJS Compiler</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/react-admin">React Admin</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/explain-new-context-api-in-react">Explain new Context API in React</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/reactjs-useeffect-hook">ReactJS useEffect Hook</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/1401-react-js-with-font-awesome">1401 React JS with Font Awesome</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/react-getting-started-tutorial">React Getting Started Tutorial</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/tailwind-css-in-react">Tailwind CSS in React</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/how-to-use-forwardref-in-react">How to Use ForwardRef in React</a></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="accordion-item" style="background-color:var(--sidebar)"><h2 class="accordion-header" id="flush-heading2"><button aria-controls="flush-collapse2" aria-expanded="true" class="accordion-button sidebar-btn" data-bs-target="#flush-collapse2" data-bs-toggle="collapse" type="button">MCQ</button></h2><div aria-labelledby="flush-heading2" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" id="flush-collapse2"><div class="accordion-body"><ul><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/reactjs-mcq">React.js MCQ</a></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="accordion-item" style="background-color:var(--sidebar)"><h2 class="accordion-header" id="flush-heading3"><button aria-controls="flush-collapse3" aria-expanded="true" class="accordion-button sidebar-btn" data-bs-target="#flush-collapse3" data-bs-toggle="collapse" type="button">Interview Questions</button></h2><div aria-labelledby="flush-heading3" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" id="flush-collapse3"><div class="accordion-body"><ul></ul></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="col-12 col-md-7"><div class="overview"><div id="city"><table><tbody><tr><td><div id="bottomnextup"><a class="next" href="react-introduction">next →</a></div><h1 class="h1">React Tutorial</h1><div style="font-size:14px;margin-top:5px;margin-bottom:5px;color:#6f6e6e"><span>14 Mar 2025</span> | <span><i aria-hidden="true" class="fa fa-clock-o"></i> 6 min read</span></div><img alt="ReactJS Tutorial" src="https://d2jdgazzki9vjm.cloudfront.net/tutorial/reactjs/images/reactjs-tutorial.png" style="float:right;padding-left:15px"><p>React is a JavaScript library for creating the Graphical User Interface (GUI) of web applications. It is used specifically for creating mobile applications and single-page web applications. The code structure in React is constructed in the form of components, which are categorized into functional components and class components. The functionality of React employs the Document Object Model (DOM), Virtual DOM, JSX (JavaScript XML), and a number of Lifecycle methods like 'render,' 'shouldComponentUpdate,' 'componentDidMount,' and 'componentWillUnmount.'</p><h2 class="h2">Features of React</h2><p>The following are some of the most significant and highly-anticipated features:</p><h3 class="h3">1) JSX</h3><p>JSX, or JavaScript XML, is an extension of the JavaScript language syntax. It allows developers to render components with syntax close to HTML. Even though React components can be constructed using pure JavaScript, JSX is employed because it is simple and simple to read. Babel is a pre-processor that transforms HTML-like syntax in JavaScript files into normal JavaScript objects. This incorporation of HTML in JavaScript makes the code easier to read, enhances the performance of JavaScript, and assists in making the overall application more stable.</p><h3 class="h3">2) Virtual Document Object Model</h3><p>React employs an in-memory data structure cache to calculate the difference between the old and new states of the DOM. It re-renders only the required changes rather than re-rendering the entire application. This selective updating process enhances speed and performance to a great extent and decreases memory usage.</p><h3 class="h3">3) Testability</h3><p>In React, views are representations of the state, which dictates the behavior of the component. Therefore, changes can be introduced to the state, which is passed to a ReactJS view in order to ascertain the resulting output, actions, functions, and events. This simplifies the process of testing and debugging.</p><h3 class="h3">4) SSR</h3><p>Server-Side Rendering (SSR) enables React applications to render content on the server prior to sending it to the browser. This technique enhances the performance of loading and search engine optimization (SEO), and thus it is extremely useful for applications that demand language translation in React. Server-side rendering of translated content enables users to view localized pages instantly without the latency of client-side translation scripts. This technique enhances performance as well as enables search engines to crawl multilingual content more efficiently.</p><h3 class="h3">5) One-Way Data Binding</h3><p>This feature enables a unidirectional flow of data, i.e., one-way data binding. This type of mechanism enables more control over the application as the state is stored in specific stores, and other components can remain loosely coupled. This structure enables the application to be more flexible and efficient.</p><h3 class="h3">6) Simplicity</h3><p>JSX files assist in keeping the application simple and easy to understand. Even though plain JavaScript can be utilized for coding, JSX offers a friendlier alternative. The presence of many lifecycle methods and a component-based system enables learning to be easy and implementation easier.</p><h3 class="h3">7) Learning Curve</h3><p>Compared to other frameworks, React has a relatively low learning curve. Individuals with basic knowledge pertaining to programming languages can learn React easily.</p><h2 class="h2">How It Works?</h2><ol><li>When the Facebook developer team was developing client-side applications, it realized that the Document Object Model (DOM) was slow. In the name of performance improvement, React utilizes a virtual DOM, i.e., a tree model of the actual DOM in JavaScript.</li><li>It is based on Virtual DOM. It never touches the browser document once changes are made; it updates the virtual DOM. When the virtual DOM has been updated to the fullest, then it updates the browser's DOM in the best possible way.</li><li>React's virtual DOM resides in memory. When it's rendering a React component, React is rendering a virtual component as a DOM representation. It's cheap to create the virtual component. It was built to run in the browser, but now with Node.js, it can run on the server as well.</li></ol><h2 class="h2">How to Use React?</h2><p>Using React is simple as including a JS file in HTML.</p><p>Let us see the usage of React as a simple example.</p><p><strong>Code</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="java" name="code">import React from 'react';
function App() {
    return (
        &lt;div&gt;
            &lt;h1&gt;Welcome To React Tutorial&lt;/h1&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
    );
}
export default App;
</textarea></div><p><strong>Output:</strong></p><img alt="ReactJS Tutorial" src="https://d2jdgazzki9vjm.cloudfront.net/tutorial/reactjs/images/reactjs-tutorial-output1.png"><h2 class="h2">Who Utilizes React?</h2><p>React is gaining traction in the current market, with its capabilities assisting major corporations in enhancing their user experiences and interfaces. Prominent internet companies such as Facebook, Instagram, Netflix, The New York Times, Yahoo Mail, Khan Academy, WhatsApp, Vivaldi Browser, Codecademy, and Dropbox have integrated React into their platforms in various capacities. Additionally, Sberbank of Russia has employed React to develop its banking website. Numerous other websites, including github.com, reddit.com, outlook.live.com, bitbucket.org, and account.godaddy.com, also leverage React.</p><h2 class="h2">Why learn React?</h2><p>React, the widely-used JavaScript library presents several compelling reasons for developers to dive into it.</p><p><strong>Flexibility</strong>: With its adaptable library approach, React enables the creation of high-quality user interfaces across various platforms.</p><p><strong>Great Developer Experience</strong>: React makes coding and reading simpler, resulting in an improved developer experience.</p><p><strong>Strong Support from Facebook:</strong> Regular updates, bug fixes, and resources from Facebook help keep React current and dependable.</p><p><strong>Vast Community Support:</strong> A large community backs React, offering a wealth of resources, tutorials, and assistance for troubleshooting.</p><p><strong>Excellent Performance:</strong> React’s efficient Virtual DOM guarantees quick rendering and outstanding performance for web applications.</p><p><strong>Easy Testing:</strong> React's structure and the tools it offers support easier testing, making code more reliable and easier to maintain.</p><h2 class="h2">Advantages of React</h2><p><strong>Composable</strong>: React makes it possible to write reusable components, which helps improve code structure and maintainability.</p><p><strong>Declarative</strong>: React, with its declarative style, makes it easier to design interactive UIs as it supports updating component states and automatically handles DOM updates.</p><p><strong>SEO-Friendly</strong>: React's SPA architecture can be optimized for Search Engine Optimization such that content can be rendered and indexed properly.</p><p><strong>Large Community:</strong> React boasts a robust community and is utilized by major companies like Meta and Netflix, making it easier to access resources and support. <strong>Easy to Learn:</strong> React employs JSX, which is similar to HTML, making it accessible for developers with a basic understanding of HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.</p><p><strong>Easy Debugging:</strong> React's unidirectional data flow makes debugging easier by having data flow predictably and straightforwardly through components.</p><h2 class="h2">Conclusion</h2><p>In conclusion, ReactJS stands out as a robust and flexible library for creating contemporary web applications. Its component-driven structure, clear syntax, and virtual DOM render it an excellent option for crafting interactive and efficient user interfaces. By grasping the fundamental principles of ReactJS and utilizing its advantages, developers can build scalable, maintainable, and high-performance web applications that cater to the needs of today’s users.</p><div class="index"><h2 class="h2">React Tutorial</h2><ul><li><a href="reactjs-tutorial">React Tutorial</a></li><li><a href="react-introduction">React Introduction</a></li><li><a href="react-version">React Version</a></li><li><a href="react-installation">React Installation</a></li><li><a href="react-create-react-app">create-react-app</a></li><li><a href="react-features">React Features</a></li><li><a href="pros-and-cons-of-react">Pros &amp; Cons</a></li><li><a href="reactjs-vs-angularjs">ReactJS vs AngularJS</a></li><li><a href="reactjs-vs-reactnative">ReactJS vs ReactNative</a></li><li><a href="react-vs-vue">React vs Vue</a></li><li><a href="react-jsx">React JSX</a></li><li><a href="react-components">React Components</a></li><li><a href="react-state">React State</a></li><li><a href="react-props">React Props</a></li><li><a href="react-props-validation">React Props Validation</a></li><li><a href="react-state-vs-props">React State vs Props</a></li><li><a href="react-constructor">React Constructor</a></li><li><a href="react-component-api">React Component API</a></li><li><a href="react-component-life-cycle">Component Life Cycle</a></li><li><a href="react-forms">React Forms</a></li><li><a href="react-controlled-vs-uncontrolled-component">Controlled vs Uncontrolled</a></li><li><a href="react-events">React Events</a></li><li><a href="react-conditional-rendering">Conditional Rendering</a></li><li><a href="react-lists">React Lists</a></li><li><a href="react-keys">React Keys</a></li><li><a href="react-refs">React Refs</a></li><li><a href="react-fragments">React Fragments</a></li><li><a href="react-router">React Router</a></li><li><a href="react-css">React CSS</a></li><li><a href="react-animation">React Animation</a></li><li><a href="react-bootstrap">React Bootstrap</a></li><li><a href="react-map">React Map</a></li><li><a href="react-table">React Table</a></li><li><a href="react-higher-order-components">Higher-Order Components</a></li><li><a href="react-code-splitting">React Code Splitting</a></li><li><a href="react-context">React Context</a></li><li><a href="react-hooks">React Hooks</a></li><li><a href="react-flux-concept">React Flux Concept</a></li><li><a href="react-flux-vs-mvc">React Flux Vs MVC</a></li><li><a href="react-redux">React Redux</a></li><li><a href="react-redux-example">React Redux Example</a></li><li><a href="react-portals">React Portals</a></li><li><a href="react-error-boundaries">React Error Boundaries</a></li></ul><h2 class="h2">Interview Questions</h2><ul><li><a href="react-interview-questions">React Interview Questions</a></li></ul></div><h2 class="h2">Prerequisite</h2><p>Before learning ReactJS in-depth, you must have a good knowledge of JavaScript, HTML5, and CSS. The knowledge of ECMAScript 2015 syntax can also be helpful.</p><h2 class="h2">Audience</h2><p>We have developed this ReactJS tutorial for beginners and professionals both. We will try to introduce every concept with simple code examples that you can be easily understood. After completing all the sections, you can work confidently with ReactJS.</p><h2 class="h2">Problems</h2><p>We assure you that you will not find any problem in our ReactJS tutorial. But, if you find any mistake, you can post it in our comment section.</p><hr/><div class="nexttopicdiv"><span class="nexttopictext">Next Topic</span><span class="nexttopiclink"><a href="react-introduction">React Introduction</a></span></div> <div id="bottomnext"><a class="next" href="react-introduction" style="float:right">next →</a></div><br> </br></img></img></td></tr></tbody></table></div><br/></div><div class="related-posts-container"><h2 class="h3">Related Posts</h2><ul class="related-posts-list"><li><a class="related-post-item" href="react-components"><h3 class="h3">React Components</h3><p class="related-post-content">Earlier, the developers write more than thousands of lines of code for developing a single page application. These applications follow the traditional DOM structure, and making changes in them was a very challenging task. If any mistake found, it manually searches the entire application and update...</p><p class="reading-time"><i aria-hidden="true" class="fa fa-clock-o"></i> 5 min read <span class="arrow-position"><svg fill="currentColor" height="1.3rem" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="0" viewbox="0 0 512 512" width="1.3rem" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M295.6 163.7c-5.1 5-5.1 13.3-.1 18.4l60.8 60.9H124.9c-7.1 0-12.9 5.8-12.9 13s5.8 13 12.9 13h231.3l-60.8 60.9c-5 5.1-4.9 13.3.1 18.4 5.1 5 13.2 5 18.3-.1l82.4-83c1.1-1